WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.060
Speaker 1: Earlier in the show I told you all&nbsp;
of the ways in which this protracted fiasco in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:10.980
the House of Representatives wherein Republicans&nbsp;
have been unable to elect a speaker is bad for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:15.120
Republicans. Now, that's good. That makes&nbsp;
me happy. I think the Republican Party is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:20.640
terrible for the country. I think their goals&nbsp;
and ambitions are destructive. I want them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:26.400
have a quagmire and to be unable to push&nbsp;
forward their disgusting agenda. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:35.580
I don't want the country to fail. And as a general&nbsp;
principle, not having a House speaker is bad for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:42.720
the country and it's a national security risk and&nbsp;
it is a source of instability on the bulwarks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:50.100
Joe per token Republican wrote an article&nbsp;
touching on some of these issues called The House&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:58.380
is Paralyzed without a speaker creating national&nbsp;
security risks and a shadow shut down. Imagine if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:05.100
this impasse were occurring in a presidential&nbsp;
inaugural year. And Joe, very precisely and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:10.440
astutely points out all of the different ways&nbsp;
in which this current status quo is very bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:15.600
including that no one has actually been sworn in&nbsp;
to the House of Representatives yet, even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.040
George Santo said he was sworn in, he wasn't.&nbsp;
And we'll get to that a little bit later. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:25.740
think it's important just to be clear about the&nbsp;
risks that this presents. It is, I would argue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:29.880
even simpler than Joe Per Tacconi lays out.&nbsp;
It's not that he's wrong, but it's even simpler.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:35.040
This situation in the House where no one&nbsp;
has been sworn in and there is no speaker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:41.220
it can lead to a functional breakdown of&nbsp;
the House of Representatives. The speaker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:48.000
actually plays a critical role in managing and&nbsp;
organizing the legislative process and with no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.820
speaker. How does the House conduct business and&nbsp;
pass legislation? Is it possible to do? It is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.820 --> 00:02:02.160
but it's significantly more of a mess within&nbsp;
government. No speaker and no one sworn in. Causes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.860
uncertainty. It causes instability. The speaker&nbsp;
is one of the most powerful and influential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:14.400
figures or positions in Congress, and the American&nbsp;
confidence in government is certain to decline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.940
The our perception of the ability of the House to&nbsp;
govern is quite low, accurately, because this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:26.940
what they're doing. And then you say, okay, fine.&nbsp;
That's that's that's abstract. But in practical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:33.000
terms, what could that mean? Well, it can lead&nbsp;
to gridlock or inaction on important issues. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:41.820
you think about debt ceiling votes or if there&nbsp;
is a natural disaster of some kind, a crisis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:49.200
an emergency, the need for funding in a specific&nbsp;
place, way or form without a speaker and without a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:54.720
sworn in house, it is significantly more difficult&nbsp;
for the House to actually manage those things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:59.940
to respond to unforeseen events. And at the end of&nbsp;
the day, to protect the country's interests. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:06.120
do I think that Republicans don't care about&nbsp;
that? No, I think at the individual level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.740
a lot of these Republicans would honestly&nbsp;
say, no, I do care about that. I understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:15.300
that I'm concerned. Does that mean they should&nbsp;
compromise on their principles to the extent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:20.460
that this situation is caused by or catalyzed&nbsp;
by their principles? No, I'm not saying that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:25.260
But we have to be factual. We have to be honest.&nbsp;
We have to be realistic. The longer this goes on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:34.320
the more instability is accurately perceived by&nbsp;
the American people. And obviously, the longer it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:41.760
goes on, the higher the chances are that something&nbsp;
could happen domestically, internationally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:49.680
and in other ways that would require, in normal&nbsp;
circumstances, the involvement of the House of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:56.220
Representatives. And they will then be chaotically&nbsp;
scrambling to try to get involved, help or do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:05.760
something about it. So. The primary interest from.&nbsp;
We have we have conflicting interests here. Each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:11.940
faction of the Republican Party wants to reign&nbsp;
supreme in terms of ultimately deciding who is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:15.300
going to become speaker of the House. I get that.&nbsp;
That's politics. They don't want to be seen as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:20.100
losers. They don't want to be seen as the ones who&nbsp;
were impotent in trying to impose their preference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:24.780
of speaker. That's one area. The impact on the&nbsp;
Republican Party we've already talked about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:31.560
On the other hand, I hope that I'm accurate in&nbsp;
assessing that the interest of the American people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:36.000
is to not have to think about the House of&nbsp;
Representatives. And what I mean by that is, yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.320
there are some people who are more politically&nbsp;
engaged than others who pay attention to. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:45.480
are the midterms? Where is control of the&nbsp;
Senate? Where is control of the House and are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:49.320
more involved in that, including people like&nbsp;
those who listen to this program, who watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:54.900
our clips? The vast majority of the country,&nbsp;
even many of those who vote in the midterms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.900 --> 00:05:00.720
don't spend their days and nights thinking about&nbsp;
this stuff. And so it's sort of like referees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:06.420
in a in a sporting event, if you're noticing the&nbsp;
referees, if you're thinking about the referees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:14.280
if the referees are significantly present in&nbsp;
the game, arguably it is because they are doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:18.840
something wrong or too involved or whatever the&nbsp;
case may be. You've heard these sports analogies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:25.020
before and likewise, I would argue if the American&nbsp;
people are thinking about the chaos in the House&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:28.560
of Representatives to the extent that it has been&nbsp;
front and center for the last two or three days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:33.000
it is a it is a bad thing. It is the type&nbsp;
of thing where we shouldn't really have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:36.840
to be thinking about it. The house is there.&nbsp;
There's a natural disaster. They're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:41.340
act. They're going to they're going to help.&nbsp;
They're going to fund. And that's where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:47.640
national security risk comes here. We now have a&nbsp;
balancing, a coming together of how long are they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:53.460
willing to continue this and how tolerant will&nbsp;
the American people be before approval rating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:57.660
not only for the House as a body where the&nbsp;
approval rating is always low, but approval for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:02.640
their individual members of Congress is going&nbsp;
to plummet? I don't know the answer to that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:05.220
but I'm guessing we're going to start&nbsp;
finding out about that very, very soon.

